<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "vital proteins collagen peptides",
      "vital proteins collagen peptides powder",
      "vital proteins review",
      "is vital proteins third-party tested",
      "vital proteins collagen protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Collagen Picks",
    link: "/best-collagen-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides Review</Fragment>
      Vital Proteins is the collagen most Americans have heard of, and its peptides now sit in the NSF Certified for Sport database. Here is what the label delivers, why collagen is not a substitute for whey, and what each gram of protein really costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Tub?</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Which Products to Buy</a>
      <a href="#complete-protein">Is Collagen a Complete Protein?</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#buyer-reviews">Taste, Mixing and Complaints</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Vital Proteins vs Whey and Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides is a clean, single-ingredient, independently certified collagen, and a poor protein powder. Per retailer labels, a 20 g serving gives about 18 g of protein and 70 calories from bovine hide, and the unflavored tub appears in NSF's Certified for Sport database. But collagen has no tryptophan and little leucine, so it complements whey rather than replacing it. Below: the label, the testing, the cost per 25 g and who should buy it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides is worth buying if you want collagen, and not worth buying if you want a protein powder. Those are different jobs. The product does its own job well: one ingredient, no sweetener in the unflavored tub, about 18 g of protein per two-scoop serving per the label, and a listing in the NSF Certified for Sport database, which is more independent verification than most collagen brands can show.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it falls down is as a substitute for whey. Collagen protein is missing tryptophan, one of the nine essential amino acids, and it carries only a fraction of the leucine that switches on muscle protein synthesis. A scoop of Vital Proteins counts towards your daily protein grams on paper but does far less for muscle than the same grams of whey, casein, egg or soy. That is a property of collagen itself, not a flaw unique to this brand.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Unflavored Collagen Peptides, per the label</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 20 g serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">70</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tryptophan (collagen has none)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.23</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein, 19.3 oz tub</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Buy it if you want a neutral, dissolvable collagen for coffee or tea, you like the reassurance of NSF certification, and your main protein already comes from food or a complete powder. Skip it if you are trying to build muscle on a budget, if you need your shake to cover a meal, or if you eat no animal products, because every Vital Proteins collagen comes from cattle hide or fish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is Vital Proteins Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. When we searched the NSF Certified for Sport database on 21 November 2026, it listed Vital Proteins LLC Collagen Peptides, plus the chocolate, vanilla, lemon and strawberry lemonade flavors and the unflavored stick packs. NSF Certified for Sport is the strictest common supplement program in the US: NSF checks that the contents match the label, screens for hundreds of substances banned in sport, and audits the factory. Fortune's 2026 review of the product reached the same conclusion.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a real point in the brand's favour. Most collagen powders, including many sold as clean or premium, carry no independent certification at all and rely on the maker's own in-house testing. If you are a drug-tested athlete, the certification is the difference between a product you can use and one you should avoid. Our guide to <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> explains how the program works and which complete proteins carry it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two caveats. First, certification is granted product by product, not to the whole brand. We did not see Marine Collagen or the Collagen Peptides Advanced tubs with hyaluronic acid and vitamin C listed under those names, so if you buy one of those, look for the NSF mark on the tub and search the lot in NSF's database rather than assuming. Second, earlier coverage on this site and elsewhere said no sport certification could be confirmed, so the listing appears to be relatively recent. Labels and listings change; check the tub you actually receive.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF certification is not a heavy-metals guarantee in the way buyers often assume. The program checks label claims and banned substances and sets contaminant limits, but NSF does not publish per-product lead or arsenic figures for consumers to compare. Vital Proteins does not publish batch heavy-metal results on its product pages either. The chocolate product page carries a California Prop 65 disclosure link, which is common for cocoa-based powders. If metals are your main worry, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">guide to protein powder without heavy metals</a> covers brands that publish numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The unflavored tub contains one ingredient: bovine hide collagen peptides. Vital Proteins describes the hides as upcycled and sourced from grass-fed, pasture-raised cattle, and retailer listings describe the collagen as types I and III, the forms found in skin, tendon and bone. A serving is two scoops, about 20 g of powder, which a Sam's Club listing puts at 70 calories and 18 g of protein. The brand's product page does not print a full nutrition panel, so these figures come from retailer copies of the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why 18 g of protein from 20 g of collagen? Nutrition panels calculate protein from nitrogen, and collagen's unusual amino acid mix, heavy in glycine, proline and hydroxyproline, plus a little residual moisture and minerals, leaves the protein figure slightly below the weight of the peptides. That is normal and not a sign of filler. The label also lists roughly 110 mg of sodium per serving on some retailer copies, which is unremarkable.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Collagen Peptides, Unflavored</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 scoops (about 20 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chocolate Collagen Peptides</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 scoops (27 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 18 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia leaf Reb M</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Collagen Peptides Advanced (HA + vitamin C)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand page doesn't state</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand page doesn't state</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None in unflavored</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Marine Collagen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand page doesn't state</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand page doesn't state</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The flavored tubs add a few ingredients. Retailer listings for the chocolate version show alkalized cocoa, natural flavors, sea salt and stevia leaf Reb M, with 0 g of sugar and 90 calories per 27 g serving. Strawberry lemonade also uses stevia, per Fortune. None of the core peptide products we checked list artificial sweeteners, and the brand markets the line as paleo and keto friendly and made without gluten or dairy. Chocolate listings note the powder is made in a facility that also processes milk, fish and tree nuts, which matters for people with severe allergies.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Advanced line adds hyaluronic acid, listed at 120 mg per serving, and 100% of the daily value of vitamin C, per retailer listings. Both ingredients are there for the skin story: vitamin C is a cofactor in the body's own collagen production, and hyaluronic acid is marketed for skin hydration. The evidence for oral hyaluronic acid is modest, and you can get vitamin C from an orange, so the Advanced tub is a convenience, not a necessity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Vital Proteins Collagen Products Are Worth Buying?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vital Proteins sells dozens of collagen products, from creamers and gummies to capsules and sparkling water. For a protein-powder shopper only the tubs of peptides matter, so these are the four worth comparing. Prices are the brand's list prices on 21 November 2026; nutrition figures come from the label or retailer listings at the time of writing and can vary by flavor and size.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="complete-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Collagen a Complete Protein? Why It Can't Replace Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. Collagen is an incomplete protein because it contains essentially no tryptophan, one of the nine essential amino acids your body cannot make. Under the PDCAAS scoring system used for protein quality, a protein missing an essential amino acid scores zero, which is why a 20 g scoop of collagen does not count towards the protein Daily Value the way whey does. Vital Proteins is not hiding this; Fortune's review and the wider literature both note it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine is the bigger practical issue. Leucine is the amino acid most strongly tied to triggering muscle protein synthesis after a meal. Whey protein is roughly 10 to 11 percent leucine, so 25 g of whey gives about 2.5 g. Collagen is closer to 3 percent, so a 20 g serving of Vital Proteins gives well under 1 g. In a 2020 study, Oikawa and colleagues found that whey, but not collagen peptides, raised muscle protein synthesis in older women, with and without resistance exercise.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make collagen useless. A 2015 trial by Zdzieblik and colleagues in older men with sarcopenia found that 15 g a day of collagen peptides alongside resistance training improved fat-free mass more than placebo, and several trials report modest improvements in skin elasticity and joint comfort. The skin and joint evidence is real but mixed, and much of it is industry-funded. Treat collagen as a targeted supplement, not as your main protein.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Count collagen grams, but do not count on them for muscle.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you take 20 g of Vital Proteins in your coffee, keep eating your full protein target from complete sources. Our <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder</a> guide walks through the amino acid gap in detail and shows how to take both.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical fix is simple. If your goal is muscle, make whey, casein, egg, soy or a pea and rice blend your main shake, and add collagen on top if you want it for skin or joints. If your goal is skin or joints and you already eat plenty of protein, collagen alone is fine. Problems only start when someone swaps a 25 g whey shake for a scoop of collagen and assumes the grams are interchangeable.
          </p>
        </div>

